      "author_association": "NONE",
      "body": "Concept and approach ACK.\n\nThe benchmark suite cleanly models standard input extractions alongside the 10 kB malleated scriptSig boundary. Looking at the micro-bench timings, the narrow band across standard inputs (~2,750 ns to 3,034 ns) makes sense: the profile is overwhelmingly dominated by elliptic curve point validation in `CPubKey::IsFullyValid()` and `XOnlyPubKey::IsFullyValid()`. Invoking `secp256k1_ec_pubkey_parse` or `secp256k1_xonly_pubkey_parse` requires decompressing the point and verifying the quadratic residue on-curve via field inversion / modular square root, which takes around 2.5 µs regardless of script type.\n\nFor `P2PKH_WorstCase`, the ~67 µs differential reflects the 291 sequential 33-byte dummy pushes. Because each push satisfies the 33-byte length filter, `Hash160` runs on every single iteration. Note also that in `GetPubKeyFromInput` (`src/common/bip352.cpp`), `std::vector<unsigned char> pubkey_candidate` is declared inside the `while` loop, so each of the 291 pushes performs a distinct heap allocation and free. Hoisting that variable outside the loop in Core would allow vector capacity reuse across pushes.\n\nOne missing dimension in the benchmark matrix is Taproot script-path spends. In `GetPubKeyFromInput`, key-path spends (`effective_size == 1`) bypass control-block parsing entirely, whereas script-path spends (`effective_size > 1`) execute control-block validation and the 32-byte `std::equal` comparison against `WitnessV1Taproot::NUMS_H`. Adding `P2TR_ScriptPath` (valid internal key, non-NUMS) and `P2TR_ScriptPath_NUMS` (skipped input) would benchmark the control block parsing overhead and the early rejection path described in BIP-352.\n",
